"Places forces supporting and restraining a proposed change in opposing groups to identify practical ways to shift the situation.\n\nUse when: Plan implementation after a change proposal has been defined. Discuss resistance and enabling conditions with affected participants.\n\nAvoid when: The audience needs a measured causal model or a customer-specific switching narrative.\n\nVisual grammar: Opposing regions contain driving and restraining forces. Directional arrows indicate whether each force supports or resists change. Length or a rating expresses assessed strength only under a declared convention.\n\nBuild: State the current condition and desired change clearly. Identify internal and external supporting and restraining forces. Assess evidence and choose actions that strengthen support or reduce constraints.\n\nCheck: Every force relates to the same proposed change. Strength ratings are explicitly subjective unless measured. Actions address the force rather than simply restating the desired outcome.\n\nPitfalls: Treating resistance as a personal defect instead of examining conditions. Adding subjective scores as if they predict success mathematically.\n\nExample: A volunteer group considering evening sessions weighs improved access against transport constraints and staff fatigue before choosing practical support measures.\n\nAlternatives: Forces of progress diagram, Impact-effort matrix."
